the legacy of storytelling in the  african-american black community is rich  in its historic this morning in  recognition of Black History Month we  hear from leading voices about the  impact of stories through history as  well as their personal experiences that  inspire them  they include civil rights icon and  Congressman John Lewis writer and  director D Reese actor Jesse Williams  writer Jacqueline Woodson writer Jason  Reynolds and actress and dancer Debbie  Allen I think the first time I realized  I was black and that it meant something  was hearing James Brown say say it loud  I'm black and I'm proud and it was like  wow yeah we can't say this I am proud it  was a moment of realizing that black was  beautiful and having come from the south  and coming from a Jim Crow South into  that moment  it was everything I saw a sign that said  white men colored men white women  colored women my skin is black I knew  that I was different we have to tell the  story we have to make it plain  [Music]  so people can feel it so people can be  inspired  [Music]  they were always barriers and you know  glass ceilings and wooden and iron doors  to get through you can't get caught up  in the the madness of hate and anger you  won't get there the creative spirit has  to live in a place of joy and light not  darkness think about the fact that I get  to be a writer born born from a lineage  of people who are not supposed to read  like how could I not be overcome with  joy and overcome with with with with  pride was illegal for us to read and  write in this country for for quite a  while for for centuries in fact and  punishable by death and far worse so we  had to be creative and figure out ways  to communicate and survive and if you're  lucky thrive and have moments of you  know fleeting joy and and and pleasure  and expression it is part of the  tradition coming all the way back from  Africa the griot was the one that kept  the history the storytellers with that  story telling the civil rights movement  would have been like a bird without  wings we understood it because we were  seeing black men lynched and hung from  trees and people water hose down when  dogs and for a young person that kind of  violence coming to you from the  authority that was terrorism the civil  rights movement was powerful and it made  you feel empowered that you stood up and  that you weren't afraid I think there  were stories actually do is they fortify  that which is the culture  the more stories that we have about us  the more that we shared them with  ourselves and with the world at large I  believe the more potent we are as a  people we've been doing this for  hundreds of years you know so this is  just using kind of like the new kind of  accessibility using the new venues to  keep telling the kind of stories that  we've always been telling it's important  to me to find ways to allow others the  opportunity to discover the humanity and  the necessity sadly for full citizenship  for black and brown folks in this  country and a tool and to do that a tool  that I can utilize to do that is  storytelling my coming to writing was  both enacted resistance because how dare  me not be on the page and and a desire  to not have my experience of spending  many years not seeing myself on the page  has happened for another young person I  grew up as spider-man Batman and you had  to kind of like find somebody to  identify with and you know it required  more imagination in a way because then  you could you can relate to qualities  versus kind of like physicality whereas  like you know when I have kids they'll  always have Black Panther and I think  that's like that's a radical position to  always have a hero that looks like them  they don't have to reach when I look  back to that moment of saying I'm black  and I'm proud and and thinking of course  I am you know of course this is the  truth I've known I wanted to be a writer  on my life but it really informed what I  was going to write and how I was going  to write it without apology I mean I was  going to write without apology about  being unapologetically black  unapologetically black I think it's so  important to know your history and tell  your story that's why I think it's great  that we did this  I wish Black History Month though was  incorporated all year yes I was doing it  wasn't just one month the shortest month  by the way 28 days as opposed to 31 that  it was incorporated to all do all of the  year that it just became a thing that  was incorporated in daily lives and in  conversation because the black  experience is fundamentally the American  experience exactly right Alex that's  right that's why roots was the story of  an American family yes and there was a  systematic effort to smash stories to  deny people their roots because without  roots you have no connection and you can  be you can be split up as families and  stories are the key to regaining  identity and that's why it's so  important to see stories and see people  that look like you that represent you  that's why Black Panther is so huge so  we can look forward to Black Panther  number 32 yeah I'll buy tickets to all  the sequels yeah it's it's a very  important conversation that was great  for more of this conversation follow at  CBS this morning on Instagram Twitter  and Facebook you'll see CBS News  correspondent sharing what black history  means to them and you can give us your  own stories using the hashtag my black  history
 

